# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <vx/loop.h>

vx_loop *vx_loop_new(void);
void vx_loop_free(vx_loop *l);
int64_t vx_loop_wait(vx_loop *l, vx_instant deadline, vx_duration leeway,
                     vx_event *evs, size_t cap);
vx_status vx_post(vx_loop *l, uint64_t a, uint64_t b);
vx_timer vx_timer_at(vx_loop *l, vx_instant at, vx_duration leeway,
                     vx_duration every, uint64_t key);
void vx_timer_stop(vx_loop *l, vx_timer t);
vx_status vx_proc_watch(vx_loop *l, vx_proc p, uint64_t key);
vx_status vx_thread_watch(vx_loop *l, vx_thread *t, uint64_t key);
vx_status vx_notes_to_loop(vx_loop *l);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A loop is one port (port(2)) and the bookkeeping that turns its packets into events, so a thread waits for everything with one call (09 §5.4, M6 step 6e4c4).
One loop per thread that wants one; one per program is usual.
`vx_post` may be called from any thread; a loop's other calls are its own thread's.

`vx_loop_wait` puts up to <cap> events in <evs>, waiting until <deadline> for the first (0, or a deadline already past, polls); <leeway> lets the wake come that much late, so one timer interrupt serves several waits.
It returns how many, 0 if the deadline came first, or a negative `vx_status`.
Every event is one 64-byte `vx_event`: its kind, the time the loop took it, the key the program gave, where it came from, and a payload.
A slice in it, an exit string or a note, points into the loop's own memory, good until its next wait.

| Kind | From | Payload |
|---|---|---|
| `VX_EV_POST` | `vx_post(l, a, b)`, any thread | `post.a`, `post.b`; the key is <a> |
| `VX_EV_TIMER` | `vx_timer_at` | `timer.id`, `timer.late` |
| `VX_EV_EXIT` | `vx_proc_watch`, `vx_thread_watch` | `exit.msg`, `""` for success; `source` is the pid or the thread |
| `VX_EV_NOTE` | a note, after `vx_notes_to_loop` | `note.text` |
| `VX_EV_IO` | a request of `vx_io_submit` (file(2)) done | `io.count`, bytes or a status; `io.fd`, `io.op`, `io.off` |
| `VX_EV_CHANGED` | a file `vx_watch` watches changed | `changed.what` (`VX_CHANGED_*`), `changed.name` |
| `VX_EV_READY` | standard input, watched, has input | `ready.fd` |

`vx_timer_at` fires at <at>, then every <every> after it (0 for once); a periodic timer that falls behind fires once, `timer.late` saying by how much, and goes on from its next time after now.
`vx_timer_stop` ends one. A loop holds 64 timers.

`vx_proc_watch` and `vx_thread_watch` give one `VX_EV_EXIT` when the process or thread ends, at once if it has; a loop holds 64 watches.
A watched thread is joined (thread(2)) after its event, which reads its record.

`vx_notes_to_loop` makes the process's notes (ADR-0010) arrive as `VX_EV_NOTE` on <l> instead of interrupting a thread: the one-wait rule applied to notes.
A trap's note (`sys: ...`) still ends the program; a wait a note interrupts goes on waiting.
Sixteen notes may wait in the loop; more are dropped.

Memory pressure and a lost server (`VX_EV_PRESSURE`, `VX_EV_HUNGUP`) have their numbers already; they come with the calls that make them.
Freeing a loop ends its watches and its I/O threads.

# EXAMPLES

```c
vx_loop *l = vx_loop_new();
vx_proc_watch(l, child, 1);
vx_timer_at(l, vx_now() + 16 * 1'000'000, 1'000'000, 16 * 1'000'000, 2);
vx_event ev[16];
for (;;) {
  int64_t n = vx_loop_wait(l, VX_INFINITE, 0, ev, 16);
  for (int64_t i = 0; i < n; i++)
    if (ev[i].kind == VX_EV_EXIT)
      return ev[i].exit.msg.len ? "child failed" : nullptr;
    else if (ev[i].kind == VX_EV_TIMER)
      tick();
}
```

